Q: What is the probability that the array will remain the same? A: <p>Here is another program to count the possibilities empirically, like <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/11875365/298225">my previous program</a>. However, that one was brute force, whereas this one exploits symmetries to count the sequences immensely faster. Output is:</p>
<pre>
n = 0, count = 1, probability = 1.
n = 1, count = 1, probability = 1.
n = 2, count = 8, probability = 0.5.
n = 3, count = 135, probability = 0.185185.
n = 4, count = 4480, probability = 0.0683594.
n = 5, count = 189125, probability = 0.0193664.
n = 6, count = 12450816, probability = 0.00571983.
n = 7, count = 935974627, probability = 0.00138004.
n = 8, count = 97730379776, probability = 0.000347208.
n = 9, count = 11189215889289, probability = 7.45477e-05.
n = 10, count = 1658208958382080, probability = 1.65821e-05.
n = 11, count = 264241964663688671, probability = 3.24611e-06.
n = 12, count = 51977759352382291968, probability = 6.53834e-07.
n = 13, count = 10873461222058959014413, probability = 1.18533e-07.
n = 14, count = 2713966083240578849177600, probability = 2.19796e-08.
n = 15, count = 715377897185751150682498875, probability = 3.73076e-09.
n = 16, count = 219334640126380638641078665216, probability = 6.44567e-10.
n = 17, count = 70673830403707739924352154391057, probability = 1.03275e-10.
n = 18, count = 25972670385405414822124510192336896, probability = 1.67767e-11.
n = 19, count = 9994784016601958786598434890390042807, probability = 2.5535e-12.
</pre>
<p>This took 56 seconds on an Apple M3.</p>
<p>(If your C implementation does not have the new <code>_BitInt</code>, you can use <code>uint64_t</code> and lower <code>MaxN</code> to 11, after which the count exceeds 2<sup>64</sup>.)</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>/*  This program counts the number of sequences of n swaps, (a1 b1) (a2 b2)… (an
    bn), that leave an n-element array of n distinct values in its original
    order.  Each swap member ai or bi is in [0, n).  Swaps are considered
    distinct for our count if they have different representations, even though
    (a0 b0) and (b0 a0) are the same permutation and (0 0) and (1 1) are the
    same permutation because they are the identity permutation.

    In the Count routine below, we partition the sequences by how many identity
    swaps they contain.  In CountNonIdentity, we characterize sequences of
    non-identity swaps by the disjoint cycles they induce in an array of n
    elements:

        The array N counts the number of cycles of each length:  N[i] is the
        number of cycles of length i.

    This is sufficient to study the effects of swaps on permutations, as
    further explained in CountNonIdentity.
*/


#include &lt;inttypes.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;


//  Define the type we use for counting large integers.
typedef unsigned _BitInt(128) UInt;


//  Set the maximum n we will explore.
static constexpr int MaxN = 19;


/*  N describes the permutations of an n-element array in terms of their
    decompositions into disjoint cycles:  N[i] is the number of disjoint cycles
    of length i.
*/
static int N[MaxN+1];


/*  CountNonIdentity return the number of ways r non-identity swaps can leave n
    array in its original order, given the current state.

    The external array N must contain a characterization of a permutation as
    described above.  In an initial call to this routine (one from another
    routine, in contrast to the recursive calls this routine makes to itself, N
    should describe the identity permutation, so all elements but N[1] are
    zero).

    n is the number of elements in the hypothetical array.

    r is the number of swaps remaining until n swaps are completed.

        r must be positive (because, just for speed, we do not handle the base
        case, where r = 0 should return 1).

        r must have the same parity as the permutations characterized in N.  (In
        particular, in an initial call to this routine, the characterization is
        for the identity permutation, which is even, so r must not be odd.)  The
        operations in this function preserve this property in recursive calls.

        Since r is positive and must be even in the initial call, 2 ≤ r in the
        initial call.

    Distance is how far we are from the identity permutation:  the number of
    swaps needed to get to the identity permutation.

        Distance = sum N[i]*(i-1).  It is easy to see Distance is at most this
        sum because i-1 swaps will undo a cycle of length i.  I have not
        drafted a proof that Distance is at least this sum.

        Distance necessarily has the same parity as r.

    MaxLength is the index of the largest element in N that is currently
    nonzero.
*/
static UInt CountNonIdentity(int n, int r, int Distance, int MaxLength)
{
    //  If we cannot return to the identity in r swaps, there are no solutions.
    if (r &lt; Distance)
        return 0;

    /*  After the initial call with 2 ≤ r, recursive calls decrease r by 1, so
        calls eventually pass through r = 2, which we fully handle here (if not
        handled above), making r = 2 the base case of this recursive function.
    */
    if (r == 2)
        /*  Handle all remaining cases when r = 2.  r &lt; Distance was handled
            above.  Distance here is 0 or 2 (parity prevents 1).

            When all the elements are in place (Distance = 0), place, we can
            keep them in place two equivalent swaps, say (a b) (b a), with a ≠
            b.  For this, we can use any of n values for a and then any of n-1
            values for b, and the swaps may appear as (a b) (b a) or
            (a b) (a b), so there are n*(n-1)*2 options.

            When we are two swaps away from the identity:

                If MaxLength is 2, we are two disjoint swaps away, and there
                are 8 ways (a b) (c d) can be arranged for the same effect, by
                swapping members within either or both swaps and/or by swapping
                the swaps.

                Otherwise, MaxLength is 3, and we are a length-3 cycle
                away, (a b c).  We can achieve that cycle with the swaps (a b)
                (c a) or (b c) (a b) or (b c) as well as those with the members
                in either or both of the swaps swapped, so 3*2*2 = 12 options.

                As it happens, 4*MaxLength provides these results correctly.
        */
        return Distance == 0 ? n*(n-1)*2 : 4*MaxLength;

    UInt C = 0;

    /*  Below, we count the results of each possible next swap:

            The swap swaps two elements in two cycles of different lengths.

            The swap swaps two elements in two cycles of the same length.

            The swap swaps two elements in the same cycle.

        By inspection of examples, we can see that swapping elements between two
        cycles of length i and j joins them into a cycle of length i+j and
        swapping two elements in one cycle breaks in into two cycles whose
        lengths sum to the original cycle's length.
    */

    /*  Consider swapping two elements in cycles of different lengths, which
        creates a new longer cycle.
    */
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= MaxLength; ++i)
    {
        //  If there are no cycles of this length, skip it.
        if (N[i] &lt; 1)
            continue;

        //  Multipliers are discussed below.
        int iMultipliers = i * 2 * N[i];

        //  We are going to lose a length-i cycle, so update N.
        N[i]   -= 1;

        for (int j = 1; j &lt; i; ++j)
        {
            //  If there are no cycles of this length, skip it.
            if (N[j] &lt; 1)
                continue;

            /*  The joined cycles will make a cycle of length i+j.  Determine
                what the new maximum length will be given this.
            */
            int M = MaxLength &lt; i+j ? i+j : MaxLength;

            /*  Here we calculate the number of swaps of an element from a
                cycle of length i with an element from a cycle of length j.  We
                can select any of the N[i] cycles of length i, any of the i
                members of that cycle, any of the N[j] cycles of length j, and
                any of the j members of that cycle.  And we can put either one
                first in the swap.  All swaps resulting from these selections
                have the same effect relative to our characterization of
                permutations.
            */
            int Multipliers = iMultipliers * j * N[j];

            //  Remove the length-j cycle from N and add the new cycle.
            N[j]   -= 1;
            N[i+j] += 1;

            /*  Count the swaps in the resulting case and multiply by the
                number of swaps that produce this case.
            */
            C += Multipliers * CountNonIdentity(n, r-1, Distance + 1, M);

            //  Undo our changes to N.
            N[i+j] -= 1;
            N[j]   += 1;
        }

        //  Restore the length-i cycle to N.
        N[i]   += 1;
    }

    /*  Consider swapping two elements in different cycles of same length,
        which creates a new longer cycle.
    */
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= MaxLength; ++i)
    {
        //  If there are not at least two cycles of this length, skip it.
        if (N[i] &lt; 2)
            continue;

        /*  We can select any of the N[i] cycles of length i first, and any
            of the i members of that cycle, then any of the N[i]-1 remaining
            cycles of that length, and any of the i members of that cycle.

            The fact we need different mulitpliers here is the reason this case
            is separated from the above case, as well as the fact we need 2 ≤
            N[i].
        */
        int Multipliers = i * i * N[i] * (N[i]-1);

        //  The joined cycles will make a cycle of length i+i.
        int M = MaxLength &lt; i+i ? i+i : MaxLength;

        //  Remove the two length-i cycles from N and add the new cycle.
        N[i]   -= 2;
        N[i+i] += 1;

        /*  Count the swaps in the resulting case and multiply by the
            number of swaps that produce this case.
        */
        C += Multipliers * CountNonIdentity(n, r-1, Distance + 1, M);

        //  Undo our changes to N.
        N[i+i] -= 1;
        N[i]   += 2;
    }

    /*  Consider swapping two elements in the same cycle, which breaks the
        cycle into two shorter cycles.

        It is possible we could make some optimizations to handle the changes to
        the maximum length better.  For example, if i &lt; MaxLength or 1 &lt;
        N[MaxLength], then the maximum length does not change when we break a
        cycle into smaller cycles.  However, some tinkering suggests
        implementing this clutters the code without providing much performance
        gain.
    */
    for (int i = 2; i &lt;= MaxLength; ++i)
    {
        //  If there are no cycles of this length, skip it.
        if (N[i] &lt; 1)
            continue;

        /*  Since j ≠ i-j, we can break any one of the N[i] length-i cycles
            into a cycle of length j and another of length i-j by selecting any
            of its i members and then the member that is j members beyond that
            (to form a length-j cycle and a length i-j cycle) or the member that
            is i-j members beyond it (to form a different length i-j cycle and a
            length j cycles.)
        */
        int Multipliers = N[i] * i * 2;

        //  We are going to lose a length-i cycle, so update N.
        N[i]   -= 1;

        /*  Explore each pair of lengths formed, j and i-j, except for j = i/2,
            which is handled below.
        */
        for (int j = 1; 2*j &lt; i; ++j)
        {
            //  Add the new shorter cycles to N.
            N[j]   += 1;
            N[i-j] += 1;

            //  Find the new maximum length.
            int M = MaxLength;
            while (N[M] == 0)
                --M;

            /*  Count the swaps in the resulting case and multiply by the
                number of swaps that produce this case.
            */
            C += Multipliers * CountNonIdentity(n, r-1, Distance - 1, M);

            //  Undo our changes to N.
            N[i-j] -= 1;
            N[j]   -= 1;
        }

        //  If i is even, consider forming two cycles of length i/2.
        if (i % 2 == 0)
        {
            int j = i/2;

            /*  As above, we can select any of the N[i] length-i cycles and any
                of the i members of that cycle.  Then the member j positions
                beyond it is determined.  Unlike above, we do not get a
                different result from selecting the member i-j positions beyond
                the first, since j = i-j.

                This is half the previous value of Multipliers.
            */
            Multipliers /= 2;

            //  Add the new shorter cycles to N.
            N[j]   += 2;

            //  Find the new maximum length.
            int M = MaxLength;
            while (N[M] == 0)
                --M;

            /*  Count the swaps in the resulting case and multiply by the
                number of swaps that produce this case.
            */
            C += Multipliers * CountNonIdentity(n, r-1, Distance - 1, M);

            //  Undo our changes to N.
            N[j]   -= 2;
        }

        //  Restore the length-i cycle to N.
        N[i]   += 1;
    }

    return C;
}


/*  Return the number of combinations of n things taken a at a time.

    This routine is called infrequently, so it is not optimized.
*/
static UInt Comb(int n, int a)
{
    if (n-a &lt; a)
        a = n-a;

    UInt P = 1;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= a; ++i)
    {
        P *= (n+1-i);
        P /= i;
    }

    return P;
}


/*  Return the n**p.

    This routine is called infrequently, so it is not optimized.
*/
static UInt Pow(int n, int p)
{
    UInt P = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; p; ++i)
        P *= n;
    return P;
}


/*  Return the number of ways r swaps can leave N in its original order, given
    the current state.

    n is the number of elements in the array.
*/
static UInt Count(int n)
{
    /*  We count by partitioning the sequences of swaps according to how many
        identity swaps they contain.

        Identity swaps, (i i), have no effect on the array and commute with
        other swaps.  Therefore the number of qualifying sequences (those that
        compose to the identity permutation) with n-r identity swaps and r
        non-identity swaps equals the number of n-r identity swaps multiplied by
        the number of qualifying sequences of r non-identity swaps multiplied by
        the number of ways to distribute the identity swaps throughout the
        non-identity swaps.

        The number of identity swaps (i i) is n.  The number of n-r identity
        swaps is n**(n-r).  The number of ways to order them among r other swaps
        is Comb(n, r).  The number of qualifying sequences of r non-identity
        swaps is provided by the Count function.

        We count only the cases where the number of non-identity swaps is even
        since an odd number of non-identity swaps cannot produce an even
        permutation, and the required permutation, the identity permutation, is
        even.
    */

    /*  Initialize N.  For each n, it starts with a representation of the
        identity permutation:  n cycles of length 1 and no other cycles.
    */
    N[1] = n;

    /*  The r == 0 case is handled separately because CountNonIdentity does not
        support r = 0 (for speed).  For that case, its count would be 1, and of
        course Comb(n, 0) is 1.
    */
    UInt C = Pow(n, n);
    for (int r = 2; r &lt;= n; r += 2)
        C += Comb(n, r) * Pow(n, n-r) * CountNonIdentity(n, r, 0, 1);

    return C;
}


#include &lt;math.h&gt;


int main(void)
{
    static constexpr UInt M = 10000000000000000000uwb;

    //  Count permutations for each n up to MaxN.
    for (int n = 0; n &lt;= MaxN; ++n)
    {
        UInt C = Count(n);
        printf(&quot;n = %d, count = &quot;, n);

        /*  The C standard does not provide a way to format _BitInt types.  So,
            if the value is small enough, we print it using uint64_t.
            Otherwise, we print its quotient modulo 10^19 followed by its
            remainder with leading zeros.
        */
        if (C &lt;= UINT64_MAX)
            printf(&quot;%&quot; PRIu64, (uint64_t) C);
        else if (C &lt;= M*UINT64_MAX)
            printf(&quot;%&quot; PRIu64 &quot;%019&quot; PRIu64,
                (uint64_t) (C/M), (uint64_t) (C%M));
        else
            printf(&quot;%&quot; PRIu64 &quot;%019&quot; PRIu64 &quot;%019&quot; PRIu64,
                (uint64_t) (C/(M*M)), (uint64_t) (C/M % M), (uint64_t) (C%M));

        printf(&quot;, probability = %g.\n&quot;, C / pow(n, 2*n));
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Detecting Browser Autofill A: <p>I tried following Lex Kazakov's answer, but the animation didn't work for me, so I figured out how to do without it. Here's a quite hacky, yet simple solution which uses the pseudo-class for Angular.</p>
<pre><code>
    input:-webkit-autofill {
      height: 41px !important; // Increase the height or some other property by one point
    }
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>
    @ViewChild('emailInput') emailInput: ElementRef&lt;HTMLInputElement&gt;;
    isAutofilled(): boolean {
      if (this.emailInput.nativeElement.offsetHeight == 41) {
        return true;
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    }
</code></pre>
